 Luxury conference, a more simple one.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 Each had a fixed cost, each had a variable cost.

12
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 These were the revenues and in each case we could look at what the break-even point was.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 In the rate of return analysis chapter we saw a break-even interest rate.

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 In the replacement analysis we were talking about a minimum market value as a break-even analysis.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 Here we will revisit the payback period.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:23,000
 We have seen the payback period before, remember, when we talked about one of the first chapters on the annual word, present word and future word.

17
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 What is a break-even analysis?

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 Basically we want to determine a value that makes two options or two elements equal.

19
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 I'm sorry but this is of course two examples we were given in the previous slide.

20
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 If we are talking about a single project or choice that we have to make, we think of the...

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 A typical example is a cost-revenue model like the one we had for the conference.

22
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 What we have is a total cost which is composed of two components, a fixed cost and a variable cost.

23
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 We have the profit or revenue minus the total cost.

24
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 This profit can also be negative of course if our total cost is larger than our revenue.

25
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 Fixed costs, we discussed them before, they do not vary with the production level or activity.

26
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 For instance the cost of a building or a fixed overhead.

27
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 Variable costs, they do change with production volume.

28
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 The more we produce, the higher these costs will be.

29
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 Think of a car, if you produce cars we need wheels, every car needs four wheels.

30
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 The more cars we make, the more wheels we will need.

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 The relationship can be linear or can be non-linear.

32
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 If it's not non-linear, it can be economies of scale.

33
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 Meaning that variable costs are going up...

34
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
 Sorry, I'm showing it on the revenue thing which is definitely not okay.

35
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 Variable costs can be non-linear, like here, sorry.

36
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:25,000
 Which means that we have economies of scale, we could have a situation which we have variable costs like this.

37
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 Where we have these economies of scale.

38
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 The breakeven point is then the point where in this case revenue and total costs are intersecting.

39
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 So if you look at this example, this is the breakeven point.

40
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 And we could do some sensitivity analysis and say, well this is the variable cost.

41
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 Suppose that we can bring it down to this, then where would our breakeven point be?

42
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 So in a sense we are doing sensitivity analysis there.

43
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
 Of course we can have non-linear relationships and then we can have more than two breakeven points.

44
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 By the way, breakeven analysis is usually a bit too simplified for real decision making.

45
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 But it can be really useful to provide some basic insights.

46
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 Very simple example, so we have a plant producing 15,000 units per month.

47
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 And we are asked to find the breakeven level with the following data.

48
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 Fixed cost of 75,000 per month, revenue of 8 dollars per unit and a variable cost of 2.5 per unit.

49
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 And we are also expected to find the expected monthly profit and loss.

50
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 Well, let's focus on this equation.

51
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 This is the profit equation, revenue minus total cost.

52
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 Revenue per unit, number of units, fixed cost, variable cost times the number of units.

53
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 And if we fill out the data we find a profit of 7,500.

54
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 By putting the profit to zero we can find an equation to find our breakeven volume.

55
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:20,000
 We could have known that if we would have done this first we would have 13,000.

56
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 Compared to the 15,000 we would have known already that we would have a profit to expect and not a loss.

57
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
 Case of Lufkin Trailer Corporation.

58
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 So it's a company that is assembling trailers per month, 30 trailers per month in one of its facilities.

59
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:56,000
 Over the last five months production dropped to 25 units per month because of the market that had a dip.

60
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 Data on fixed cost, revenue, variable cost or given.

61
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
 We are asked to answer three questions.

62
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:11,000
 To compare the current production level of 25 units per month with the current breakeven point.

63
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:22,000
 To calculate the current profit and then to see what is the difference if the fixed cost remains constant.

64
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 What is the difference needed between revenue and variable cost to breakeven at the production level of 15 units.

65
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
 So basically this is nothing new, it's very similar to the previous one.

66
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
 Again, well we have this equation to compute our breakeven.

67
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:53,000
 Here we can compute our profit and if we use in this equation we fill out a Q of 15.

68
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 We can find what they ask for, the difference between the R and the V being the revenue and the variable cost per unit.

69
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
 So again pretty straightforward.

